SELECT 'Generative AI for Social Media Marketing: From Empty Calendar to Campaign That Performs', 'generative-ai-social-media-marketing-campaigns', CONCAT('The hard part of social media was never writing the post ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' it was sustaining a consistent presence on the right platforms with something worth saying. Generative AI fixes the sustainability problem: platform selection, a month of on-brand posts and visuals from one brief, and performance analysis that feeds the next month. Plus the WSQ course, funded up to 70%.'),
  '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> Almost nobody abandons social media because writing one post is hard. They abandon it because writing 60 posts a quarter, on brand, on the right platforms, while doing their actual job, is unsustainable &mdash; so the account goes quiet, and a quiet account performs worse than no account. Generative AI addresses precisely that failure: one campaign brief becomes a month of platform-appropriate posts, visuals and ad copy, and the performance data feeds the next month. What it does not do is decide what you stand for or which platform deserves your effort. This article covers both halves. The hands-on version is the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-generative-ai-for-social-media-marketing.html">WSQ Generative AI for Social Media Marketing</a> course (TGS-2021003023), funded up to 70%.</p> <h2>Consistency is the whole game</h2> <p>Social platforms reward accounts that post regularly with audiences that engage. Both halves of that sentence punish the stop-start pattern most small teams fall into: a burst of enthusiasm, three weeks of daily posts, then silence when a client deadline lands. The audience that was starting to recognise you moves on, the algorithm deprioritises the account, and the next burst starts from a lower base.</p> <p>This is why the productivity argument for generative AI in social is stronger than it looks. It is not &ldquo;save ten minutes per post&rdquo;. It is &ldquo;make a sustainable cadence possible for a team that could not otherwise sustain one&rdquo; &mdash; and cadence is what the platforms actually reward.</p> <h2>Step one: choose platforms before creating anything</h2> <p>Being present on six platforms badly is worse than two platforms well, because each account carries a maintenance cost and a quality expectation. The evaluation is uncomfortable but quick:</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Why it decides the answer</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Is our buyer actually here?</td><td>A B2B service selling to finance directors does not need a TikTok presence to be credible</td></tr> <tr><td>Can we produce this format sustainably?</td><td>Short-form video demands a production habit; if you cannot sustain it, do not start</td></tr> <tr><td>Does the platform still send traffic?</td><td>Reach and link behaviour change; last year''s answer may be wrong</td></tr> <tr><td>Is anyone here converting today?</td><td>Check your own analytics before your assumptions</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Two platforms done properly, with a real cadence and a distinct approach on each, beats six accounts running the same auto-posted text. The course covers this evaluation explicitly because it is the decision that makes everything downstream cheaper.</p> <h2>Step two: one brief, a month of content</h2> <p>The workflow that changes a team''s output is generating a month at once from a single brief, rather than improvising daily. Roughly:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Write the brief</strong> &mdash; the campaign objective, the audience, the three or four messages you want to land, the offer, and the tone. This is the human work and it takes an hour.</li> <li><strong>Generate the month</strong> &mdash; ask for a calendar with a mix of formats (educational, proof, behind-the-scenes, offer) mapped across the weeks, then the posts themselves per platform, with the platform''s conventions respected rather than one text reposted everywhere.</li> <li><strong>Generate the visuals</strong> &mdash; AI image tools for consistent, on-brand graphics, or briefs for a designer where the stakes justify it.</li> <li><strong>Edit ruthlessly.</strong> Expect to rewrite roughly a third. That is a good ratio, and it is much faster than starting from nothing.</li> <li><strong>Schedule, then leave room</strong> &mdash; keep two or three slots open per week for reactive posts, because the timely ones usually perform best.</li> </ol> <p>The trap to avoid is scheduling a month of content and then disappearing. Social is a conversation; replies and comments are where relationships form, and no scheduler covers that.</p> <h2>Making AI posts sound like you, not like AI</h2> <p>Audiences have become good at spotting generic AI copy, and the tells are consistent: opening with a rhetorical question, hedged conclusions, tidy lists of three, an emoji at the start of every line, and no opinion anywhere. Four fixes, in order of effect:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Feed it your best past posts</strong> as voice examples. This does more than any tone adjective.</li> <li><strong>Ban your tells explicitly</strong> in the instruction &mdash; no opening rhetorical questions, no &ldquo;in today''s fast-paced world&rdquo;, no emoji in the first line.</li> <li><strong>Insist on a point of view.</strong> A post that could have been written by any competitor is not worth publishing.</li> <li><strong>Keep a specific detail in every post</strong> &mdash; a number, a name, a real situation. Specificity is the fastest cure for generic.</li> </ul> <h2>Repurposing: one asset, a fortnight of posts</h2> <p>The most underused move in social is not generating new ideas &mdash; it is mining the assets you already have. A single substantial piece of work contains a fortnight of posts if you break it apart deliberately, and generative AI is very good at that decomposition because it is a restructuring task rather than an inventing one.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Source asset</th><th>What it yields</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>A long blog post or guide</td><td>Three or four standalone tips, one contrarian take, a carousel of the key steps, a quote graphic</td></tr> <tr><td>A customer case study</td><td>A before-and-after post, the specific obstacle they hit, the result with a real number, a lesson others can apply</td></tr> <tr><td>A webinar or talk recording</td><td>Short clips, the audience questions as individual posts, the one slide people photographed</td></tr> <tr><td>Your own FAQ or support queue</td><td>A post per genuine question &mdash; the highest-converting social content most businesses never make</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Ask for the decomposition explicitly: <em>&ldquo;From this guide, give me eight standalone social posts. Each must make one point that stands on its own without the guide, keep a specific detail from the source, and end differently &mdash; do not use the same call to action eight times.&rdquo;</em> That final clause matters, because the default behaviour is eight posts with identical closings, which reads as automation the moment someone sees two of them.</p> <h2>Ads: where the copy work pays immediately</h2> <p>Paid social is where generative AI has the clearest measurable payoff, because ad platforms reward volume of variants and you can attribute the result. Generate ten headline and body combinations against one offer, run them, and let the data pick &mdash; a process that used to stall because writing ten variants was tedious. Keep the claims accurate and compliant with <a href="https://asas.org.sg/">Singapore advertising standards</a>; the platform''s approval process is not a substitute for your own review.</p> <h2>Measuring what actually matters</h2> <p>Vanity metrics are the enemy of good social strategy. Impressions and follower counts feel like progress and rarely correlate with revenue. The metrics worth reviewing monthly: engagement rate by post type (which tells you what to make more of), click-through to your site, conversions from social in <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/9304153">Analytics</a>, and cost per result for anything paid. AI is useful for the consolidation and the first-pass read &mdash; ask it to identify which post types outperformed and what they had in common, then decide what that means for next month''s brief.</p> <h2>Learn it hands-on, funded up to 70%</h2> <p>The <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-generative-ai-for-social-media-marketing.html">WSQ &ndash; Generative AI for Social Media Marketing</a> course (TGS-2021003023) is a two-day programme that builds a full campaign. Its four topics:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Plan a social media marketing campaign with generative AI</strong> &mdash; a data-driven plan, audience analysis and objectives.</li> <li><strong>Evaluate social media marketing opportunities and platforms</strong> &mdash; choosing where to be present and why.</li> <li><strong>Create a social media marketing campaign with generative AI</strong> &mdash; posts, visuals and ad copy with tools including ChatGPT and AI image and video generators.</li> <li><strong>Evaluate social media marketing performance with generative AI</strong> &mdash; tracking, optimisation and assessing impact on the organisation.</li> </ol> <p>Assessment is a written and a practical exam.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Funding</th><th>What you pay (incl. 9% GST)</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Full course fee</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>S$720.00 before GST (S$64.80 GST)</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above</td><td>50% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$424.80</strong></td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored SG/PR staff</td><td>70% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$280.80</strong></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Eligible Singaporeans can offset the nett fee with <a href="https://www.skillsfuture.gov.sg/">SkillsFuture Credit</a> or PSEA, and eligible Singapore-registered companies can tap SkillsFuture Enterprise Credit (SFEC); SME employers can also claim absentee payroll. The course runs over two full days (9:30am to 6:30pm), with physical classroom and synchronous online (Zoom) options. Fees are as published on the course page at the time of writing &mdash; the live page always carries the current fee and the next available dates.</p> <p>To go further, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-social-media-marketing.html">WSQ Agentic AI for Social Media Marketing</a> automates the campaign loop with AI agents, and <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-tiktok-marketing.html">WSQ Agentic AI for TikTok Marketing</a> covers short-form video specifically.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How many platforms should a small business be on?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Usually two, done properly. Each account carries a maintenance cost and a quality expectation, and a neglected account signals more than an absent one. Choose based on where your buyers are, what formats you can sustain, and what your own analytics say is already converting.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How do I stop AI-generated social posts sounding generic?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Feed it your best past posts as voice examples, ban your specific tells in the instruction, insist on a point of view rather than balanced neutrality, and require a concrete detail in every post &mdash; a number, a name, a real situation. Expect to rewrite about a third of what comes back.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Can AI generate images for social media too?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes, and it is genuinely useful for consistent on-brand graphics at volume. Keep a house style so the set looks like one brand, check anything depicting people or products carefully, and use a designer where the stakes justify it.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is it safe to schedule a whole month of posts in advance?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Schedule most of it, but keep two or three slots free each week for timely posts, and stay present in the comments. Social is a conversation &mdash; a fully automated account that never replies performs worse than a smaller, responsive one.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Which social metrics actually matter?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Engagement rate by post type, click-through to your site, conversions from social, and cost per result on paid. Impressions and follower counts feel like progress but rarely track revenue.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What funding applies?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>TGS-2021003023 is WSQ funded at 50% for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above and 70% for Singaporeans aged 40 and above or SME-sponsored staff, with SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA and SFEC available against the nett fee.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>The bottom line</h2> <p>Social media rewards consistency, and consistency is exactly what small teams cannot sustain by hand. Choose two platforms deliberately, build a month from one brief, edit hard enough that it sounds like you, keep slots free for the timely posts, and measure the metrics that connect to revenue. Generative AI makes that cadence achievable &mdash; the strategy and the personality still have to be yours.</p> <p><strong>Ready to sustain a real presence?</strong> <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-generative-ai-for-social-media-marketing.html">Register for WSQ &ndash; Generative AI for Social Media Marketing</a> &mdash; 2 days, funded up to 70%, SkillsFuture Credit claimable.</p>',
  'Dr. Alfred Ang', 1, '2025-05-27', 'TGS-2021003023', 'TGS-2021003023',
  'Generative AI for Social Media Marketing: Plan, Create, Measure | WSQ Course',
  CONCAT('Use generative AI across social media marketing ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' platform evaluation, campaign planning, AI-generated posts, visuals and ad copy, and performance analysis ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' with the WSQ Generative AI for Social Media Marketing course (TGS-2021003023) funded up to 70%.'),
  'generative ai social media marketing, ai social media content, ai content calendar, social media campaign ai, ai image generation marketing, social media course singapore, wsq social media marketing, skillsfuture social media course, ai ad copy, social media analytics ai, content repurposing ai, brand voice ai',
  133, 'manual-skip', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW()
FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg = 1
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT post_id FROM mmd_blog_post WHERE url_key = 'generative-ai-social-media-marketing-campaigns') x);

INSERT INTO tag (name, status, first_store_id)
SELECT 'Generative AI', 1, 0 FROM DUAL
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT tag_id FROM tag WHERE name = 'Generative AI') x);

